Relation Bound Markets, in order Basket <code>implication</code> P(A) ≤ P(B) when A implies B A, B NO on A + YES on B, pays at least 1 <code>exclusivity</code> probabilities sum to at most 1 every outcome NO on every outcome, pays at least n − 1 <code>complement</code> P(A) + P(B) = 1 A, B YES + YES or NO + NO, pays 1 <code>same_event</code> P(A) = P(B), one event on two venues A, B YES on one + NO on the other, pays 1 <code>event_sum</code> probabilities sum to 1 for mutually exclusive, exhaustive outcomes every outcome YES on all (pays 1) or NO on all (pays n − 1) <p>Whether a relation holds is a judgment about the contracts' wording and resolution rules. Read both markets with <code>get_market</code> and obtain any missing rules before relying on a result.</p> <p>Both check tools return <code>best</code> (or <code>null</code> when no candidate can be priced), <code>missing_quotes</code>, <code>assumptions</code> and, on current main, <code>assessment</code>:</p> <pre><code>{
  "relation_verified": false,
  "execution_verified": false,
  "conditional_on_relation": true
}
</code></pre> <p><code>profitable_after_fees</code> describes conditional arithmetic. The tools do not verify settlement equivalence, order-book depth or full fills. <code>check_constraint_live</code> fetches current quotes and fee terms but retains the same assessment boundary.</p>
